### When to Use Switch
When comparing a single integer or character variable against multiple constant values, a `switch` statement provides cleaner, more readable code than a ladder of `if-else if` blocks. Furthermore, compilers can optimize switches into **jump tables** (O(1) dispatch).

### Anatomy of a Switch Statement
```c
switch (expression) {
    case CONST_1:
        // Statements
        break;
    case CONST_2:
        // Statements
        break;
    default:
        // Executed if no case matched
        break;
}
```

### The Fallthrough Phenomenon
Unlike modern languages that automatically break between cases, C falls through to subsequent cases unless an explicit `break;` is encountered. While often a source of bugs, intentional fallthrough allows grouping multiple cases:
```c
switch (errorCode) {
    case 401:
    case 403:
        printf("Access Denied\n");
        break;
    case 404:
        printf("Resource Not Found\n");
        break;
}
```

### Constraints of Switch
- The switch expression MUST evaluate to an integer or character type (floats and strings are prohibited).
- Case labels must be compile-time constants (literals or `#define` macros).

