### Emulating Pass-by-Reference
Because C strictly evaluates parameters by value, a standard function receives duplicate copies of arguments. To permit a function to modify caller variables, we pass the **addresses** of those variables:

```c
// Classic Swap Function
void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10, y = 20;
    swap(&x, &y); // Passes memory addresses!
    // Now x == 20, y == 10
}
```

### Multiple Return Values
In C, a function can only have one return type in its signature. To return multiple values from a function, pass pointers to output variables into the parameter list:
```c
void getMinMax(int a, int b, int *min, int *max) {
    if (a < b) {
        *min = a;
        *max = b;
    } else {
        *min = b;
        *max = a;
    }
}
```

